# Runbook: Recovering from Loss of Two Control-Plane Nodes (etcd Quorum Loss)
## OpenShift 4.20 · Bare Metal · Unmanaged BareMetalHosts

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Cluster | `3novvt` (`*.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local`) |
| OCP version | 4.20.21 |
| Platform | BareMetal, BMH `unmanaged` (no BMC credentials), Machine API objects present |
| Control-plane nodes | `master01`, `master02`, `master03` |
| Machines | `3novvt-csrrj-master-0`, `-master-1`, `-master-2` (namespace `openshift-machine-api`) |
| Bastion / `oc` host | `bastion02` |
| Scenario | **Two** control-plane nodes failed. One node still has an intact OS disk and etcd data. |
| Companion document | *OCP4-Control-Plane-Node-Replacement-Runbook.md* (single-node replacement) — referenced below as **Runbook A** |
| Expected duration | 2–3 hours (10 min to restore API; the rest is rebuilding two nodes sequentially) |
| Impact | Until Phase 1 completes the API is read-only or down; workloads already running keep running. Writes made in the last seconds before failure may be lost. |

Throughout this document the examples assume **master03 survived** and **master01 + master02 failed**. Swap names to match reality.

---

## 0. Decision tree — confirm this is the right procedure

```
How many control-plane nodes are healthy (OS boots, disk intact)?
│
├─ 2 healthy, 1 failed ──────────────► Use Runbook A (single-node replacement). STOP here.
│
├─ 1 healthy, 2 failed ──────────────► THIS RUNBOOK (quorum-restore.sh).
│
└─ 0 healthy ────────────────────────► Appendix B (rebuild one master, restore from etcd snapshot
                                        with cluster-restore.sh). Requires a prior backup.
```

Checks to classify the state (run from bastion02; the API may be slow or read-only):

```bash
oc get nodes                                   # may time out — that is itself a sign of quorum loss
oc get pods -n openshift-etcd -l app=etcd --field-selector=status.phase==Running -o wide
ping -c2 master01.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local
ping -c2 master02.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local
ping -c2 master03.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local
ssh core@master03.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local 'sudo crictl ps | grep etcd; sudo ls /var/lib/etcd/member'
```

If `oc` commands hang entirely, use the surviving node directly:

```bash
ssh core@master03.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local
sudo -i
export KUBECONFIG=/etc/kubernetes/static-pod-resources/kube-apiserver-certs/secrets/node-kubeconfigs/localhost.kubeconfig
oc get nodes
```

