## 1. Pre-flight checklist

- [ ] Exactly **one** control-plane node is up and its `/var/lib/etcd/member` directory exists and is non-empty.
- [ ] The two failed nodes are **powered off or fenced** and will stay off until Phase 2. Rebooting one of them mid-procedure can create a split etcd cluster.
- [ ] SSH as `core` to the surviving node works (`ssh core@master03…`).
- [ ] Replacement hardware (or repaired servers) for the two failed nodes is available with the **same hostnames and IPs**. Note whether NIC MACs changed.
- [ ] Out-of-band console / virtual-media access to both failed hosts.
- [ ] Original network keyfiles (`m1-*.nmconnection`, `m2-*.nmconnection`) are on the bastion.
- [ ] Bastion can download a 4.20 RHCOS live ISO and `coreos-installer` (or they are already cached from Runbook A).
- [ ] Incident / change record opened; stakeholders informed that the API is unavailable until Phase 1 completes.

Capture evidence before changing anything:

```bash
date -u
oc get clusterversion 2>&1 | head
oc get nodes -o wide 2>&1
oc get machines,bmh -n openshift-machine-api 2>&1
ssh core@master03.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local 'sudo crictl ps -a | grep -E "etcd|kube-apiserver"'
```

---

## 2. Phase 1 — Restore etcd quorum on the surviving node

### 2.1 Choose the recovery node

With only one node alive there is no choice: the survivor is the recovery node. If, unexpectedly, more than one etcd pod is reachable, pick the member with the **highest Raft index** that is **not** a learner:

```bash
oc exec -n openshift-etcd <etcd-pod> -c etcdctl -- etcdctl endpoint status -w table
```

### 2.2 Snapshot the surviving data (best effort — do not skip if it works)

```bash
ssh core@master03.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local
sudo -i
mkdir -p /home/core/assets/pre-quorum-restore
cp -a /var/lib/etcd/member /home/core/assets/pre-quorum-restore/member-$(date +%F-%H%M)
ls -l /home/core/assets/pre-quorum-restore/
```

This raw copy is your fallback if the restore script misbehaves.

### 2.3 Run the quorum-restore script

Still on master03 as root:

```bash
sudo -E /usr/local/bin/quorum-restore.sh
```

What it does: stops the etcd static pod, rewrites the local member as a brand-new single-member cluster (new cluster ID, old peers invalidated) using the existing data directory, and restarts etcd. Typical runtime 2–5 minutes. Watch the output for `etcd container is running` / completion messages.

```bash
exit   # leave root
exit   # leave SSH
```

### 2.4 Confirm the API is back in read/write mode (from bastion02)

```bash
# can take 3–10 minutes
watch -n 10 'oc get nodes; echo; oc get pods -n openshift-etcd -l app=etcd'
```

Expected:

- `oc get nodes` returns quickly; master03 `Ready`, master01/master02 `NotReady`.
- Exactly one `etcd-master03…` pod `Running` (4/4).
- A write works:

  ```bash
  oc create configmap quorum-test -n default && oc delete configmap quorum-test -n default
  ```

Operators will be Degraded (1 of 3 members). That is expected until Phase 2 is done.

```bash
oc get co etcd kube-apiserver kube-controller-manager kube-scheduler
oc rsh -n openshift-etcd etcd-master03.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local etcdctl member list -w table   # 1 member
```

> **Checkpoint 1 reached:** cluster is single-master but writable. Workload impact is over; the rest of the procedure restores HA.

---

## 3. Phase 2 — Rebuild the two failed control-plane nodes (one at a time)

Do **master01 first, fully, then master02**. Parallel rebuilds make failures impossible to attribute and risk the etcd operator adding two members at once.

> Never delete or re-create the Machine/Node of the **recovery host (master03)**.

### 3.1 Clean up cluster objects for the failed node

(`<NODE>` = `master01.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local`, `<MACHINE>` = `3novvt-csrrj-master-0`)

```bash
NODE=master01.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local
MACHINE=3novvt-csrrj-master-0

# etcd secrets for the dead member (operator will not re-add a same-named member while these exist)
oc delete secret -n openshift-etcd etcd-peer-$NODE etcd-serving-$NODE etcd-serving-metrics-$NODE --ignore-not-found
oc get secrets -n openshift-etcd | grep $NODE          # must be empty

# Node, then Machine (Node first, or the Machine delete hangs on drain)
oc delete node $NODE
oc delete machine $MACHINE -n openshift-machine-api
```

If the Machine delete hangs > 2 minutes:

```bash
oc annotate machine $MACHINE -n openshift-machine-api machine.openshift.io/exclude-node-draining=true
```

Verify the BareMetalHost was released and (if hardware changed) fix the MAC:

```bash
oc get bmh $NODE -n openshift-machine-api -o jsonpath='consumerRef={.spec.consumerRef}{"\n"}bootMAC={.spec.bootMACAddress}{"\n"}'
# consumerRef must be empty
# oc patch bmh $NODE -n openshift-machine-api --type=merge -p '{"spec":{"bootMACAddress":"<NEW_MAC>"}}'
```

No `etcdctl member remove` is required in this scenario — the quorum-restore script already discarded the old membership.

### 3.2 Re-create the Machine object

```bash
oc get machine 3novvt-csrrj-master-2 -n openshift-machine-api -o yaml > $MACHINE.yaml
```

Edit `$MACHINE.yaml`:

1. `metadata.name: 3novvt-csrrj-master-0`
2. Remove `status:` entirely.
3. Remove `spec.providerID`.
4. Remove `metadata.uid`, `resourceVersion`, `creationTimestamp`, `generation`, `annotations`, `finalizers`.
5. Keep `metadata.labels` and `spec.providerSpec`.

```bash
oc apply -f $MACHINE.yaml
sleep 30
oc get machine $MACHINE -n openshift-machine-api                                   # Provisioning
oc get bmh $NODE -n openshift-machine-api -o jsonpath='{.spec.consumerRef.name}{"\n"}'   # 3novvt-csrrj-master-0
```

### 3.3 Build the ISO for this node (on bastion02)

```bash
mkdir -p ~/cp-rebuild && cd ~/cp-rebuild

# fresh master ignition from the cluster — never the one from the original install dir
oc extract -n openshift-machine-api secret/master-user-data-managed --keys=userData --to=- > master.ign
python3 -m json.tool master.ign >/dev/null && echo "ignition OK"

# 4.20 live ISO (once) — URL from: openshift-install coreos print-stream-json | grep '\.iso[^.]'
[ -f rhcos-4.20-live.x86_64.iso ] || wget <ISO_URL> -O rhcos-4.20-live.x86_64.iso
[ -x coreos-installer ] || { wget https://mirror.openshift.com/pub/openshift-v4/x86_64/clients/coreos-installer/latest/coreos-installer_amd64 -O coreos-installer; chmod +x coreos-installer; }

# master01 — single NIC example (use bond + port keyfiles if that host used a bond)
./coreos-installer iso customize --dest-device /dev/sda \
  --dest-ignition master.ign \
  --network-keyfile m1-<IFNAME>.nmconnection \
  -o master01-rebuild.iso rhcos-4.20-live.x86_64.iso
```

Keyfile checks: same interface name, IP, prefix, gateway, DNS, MTU, and the static route to the subnet where HAProxy/`api-int` lives. Confirm `/dev/sda` is the OS disk on the target host (`lsblk` from the plain live ISO if unsure — `--dest-device` wipes it without prompting).

### 3.4 Install the node

1. Attach `master01-rebuild.iso` via virtual media, boot from it.
2. Unattended install → reboot. **Eject the ISO.**
3. First disk boot pulls full config from `api-int…:22623`, pivots to the 4.20.21 OS image, reboots again (10–20 min).

Monitor:

```bash
watch -n 15 'oc get csr | grep -i pending; echo; oc get nodes'
```

### 3.5 Approve CSRs (two rounds)

```bash
oc get csr -o name | xargs oc adm certificate approve
# wait ~2 min
oc get csr -o name | xargs oc adm certificate approve
oc get nodes          # master01 Ready, roles control-plane,master
```

### 3.6 Wait for etcd to add the member before touching the next node

```bash
watch -n 20 'oc rsh -n openshift-etcd etcd-master03.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local etcdctl member list -w table 2>/dev/null; echo; oc get pods -n openshift-etcd -l app=etcd -o wide'
```

Proceed only when the member list shows **2 started members** and the `etcd-master01…` pod is `Running` (allow up to 15 min). If stuck, see §5.

> **Checkpoint 2 reached:** two healthy masters, quorum (2/2) restored.

### 3.7 Repeat §3.1–3.6 for master02

```bash
NODE=master02.3novvt.samsungcnfcu.local
MACHINE=3novvt-csrrj-master-1
```

Keyfile `m2-<IFNAME>.nmconnection`, ISO name `master02-rebuild.iso`. Wait for **3 started members** at the end.

