## Appendix B — All three control-plane nodes lost (restore from snapshot)

Only if **no** master has an intact etcd data directory. Requires a `snapshot_*.db` + `static_kuberesources_*.tar.gz` pair from a previous `cluster-backup.sh` run.

1. Rebuild **one** master (e.g. master03) from ISO exactly as in §3.3–3.4. It will not join anything yet (no API), but it will boot RHCOS with the master ignition content baked in.
2. Copy the backup directory to it: `scp -r etcd-backup-<date>/ core@master03…:/home/core/assets/backup/`
3. On that node: `sudo -E /usr/local/bin/cluster-restore.sh /home/core/assets/backup`
4. `sudo systemctl restart kubelet`; wait for kube-apiserver to come up; approve CSRs for the node.
5. Follow the OpenShift 4.20 documentation *"Restoring to a previous cluster state"* for the remaining steps (force redeploy of etcd, kube-apiserver, kube-controller-manager, kube-scheduler; re-create the other two masters; recover expired certificates if the outage was long).
6. All cluster state after the snapshot time is lost; re-apply from Git/CI.

